    kuna issue testcase: the i386 PIC base register folded into the
    cross-reference index (`option picbase on|off`, default-ON).

    Issue:      kuna-analysis-picbase - `kuna strings` (JSON, filtered to Password)
                on crackmes.one/5ab77f6333c5d40ad448ca52 (pancrackme v1.0,
                32-bit i386 PIC ELF, stripped) found the prompt at 0x8049127
                and reported xrefs_count 0 with an empty functions list, as did
                all 23 of that image's strings.  The address is formed at run
                time - `call 0x80489f4; pop ebx; add ebx,0x184c` makes ebx the
                GOT pointer and `lea eax,[ebx-0x1119]` is the prompt - so the
                literal 0x8049127 occurs nowhere in the file and no scan over
                decode-time constants can find it.
    Stage:      P1 code/data partition - the on-demand cross-reference query
                (`listing/xrefs.rs`), the data-reference projection.
    P0 assert:  `option picbase on|off`.

    The Listing tier and the xref query it feeds are built ONLY on the
    real-object bootstrap path, never on this XML binaryimage path
    (docs/spec/01-program-prep.md section 1.6), which is exactly why the parity
    oracle is structurally untouched by this change.  On this path the option is
    therefore INERT, and that is what this case pins - the same contract
    `kuna-unmappedentry.xml` and `kuna-listing-flag.xml` pin for their options:

      * `option picbase off` PARSES and round-trips (an unknown option would
        make the console error and the second decompile would not run),
      * flipping it changes NOTHING about the rendered C, in both directions.

    The end-to-end two-pass proof (option off = every literal referenced by
    nothing, default = the prompt, the banner, the thunked trailer and the
    global all resolved, and the clobbering function still claiming nothing)
    needs a real 32-bit PIC object and lives in the cargo gate
    `decompiler/crates/kuna-console/tests/verify_picbase.rs`, over the
    `picbase_i386` fixture.

    Reuses the GH-558 bytes as a small, deterministic loadable function.

    Source:           int gh558(int x) { if (x LE 8) return 1; return 0; }
    Compiled (as):    cmp $8,%edi ; jg +6 ; mov $1,%eax ; ret ; xor %eax,%eax ; ret
-->
